// Dehaze, step 1 of 2 — the DARK CHANNEL (Phase 8, Lightroom "Presence").
// The third custom Core Image kernel in this codebase (after
// SelectiveColor.cikernel and ColorRange.cikernel). Core Image ships no
// dehaze filter of any kind, and no built-in can compute a per-pixel
// cross-channel minimum normalized by a second image.
//
// Implements the dark channel from He, Sun & Tang, "Single Image Haze Removal
// Using Dark Channel Prior" (CVPR 2009). The prior: in a haze-free outdoor
// image, most non-sky patches contain at least one colour channel with a very
// low value. Where that minimum is HIGH instead, the patch is hazy — so the
// dark channel is a direct estimate of haze density.
//
// This kernel does the PER-PIXEL half only: min over the three channels of
// I/A. The SPATIAL half (min over a patch) is CIMorphologyMinimum applied to
// this kernel's output — see EditPipeline.applyDehaze. Splitting it that way
// is what lets a CIColorKernel (which cannot sample neighbours) implement an
// inherently neighbourhood-based prior.
//
// atmos is the atmospheric light A, delivered as a CONSTANT FIELD: a 1x1
// CIAreaMaximum result spread with .clampedToExtent(). That keeps A on the
// GPU — a CPU readback here would stall every frame.
//
// Output is vec4(d,d,d,1): opaque on purpose, so the CIMorphologyMinimum that
// follows cannot pull alpha down and darken the frame's own edges.

kernel vec4 dehazeDarkChannel(__sample s, __sample atmos) {
    // Unpremultiply before the maths, matching SelectiveColor/ColorRange.
    float a = s.a;
    vec3 I = (a > 0.0) ? s.rgb / a : s.rgb;

    float aa = atmos.a;
    vec3 A = (aa > 0.0) ? atmos.rgb / aa : atmos.rgb;

    // A is a DIVISOR: guard it away from zero. A near-black frame (or a fully
    // transparent one) would otherwise divide by ~0 and produce inf/NaN that
    // propagates through the morphology pass into every pixel.
    A = max(A, vec3(0.0001));

    // Clamp negatives before dividing. This working space is
    // extendedLinearSRGB and genuinely carries values below 0 (see
    // ColorRange.cikernel's own note) — a negative channel would otherwise
    // make the dark channel negative and drive transmission above 1.
    vec3 n = max(I, vec3(0.0)) / A;

    float d = min(n.r, min(n.g, n.b));
    return vec4(d, d, d, 1.0);
}
